Q: UI components initially the wrong color when sheet is launched in iOS26 A: <p>The appearance of the <code>Button</code> and <code>Picker</code> seem to depend on the sheet background and, with iOS 26, the sheet background depends on the size of the sheet. Apple's notes about <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/TechnologyOverviews/adopting-liquid-glass#Windows-and-modals" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Adopting Liquid Glass</a> explain more:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Modal views like sheets and action sheets adopt Liquid Glass. Sheets feature an increased corner radius, and half sheets are inset from the edge of the display to allow content to peek through from beneath them. When a half sheet expands to full height, it transitions to a more opaque appearance to help maintain focus on the task.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you apply <code>.presentationDetents([.medium, .large])</code> to the sheet content in your example and run on an iPhone 17 simulator, you will see how the components change appearance when switching between the two sheet sizes. The disabled <code>Button</code> should be moved inside the <code>VStack</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>.sheet(isPresented: $sheetIsPresented) {
    VStack {
        // ... Picker and Button
    }
    .presentationDetents([.medium, .large])
}
</code></pre>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/gw8Fq9qI.gif" alt="Animation" /></p>
<hr />
<p>As a workaround, try setting <code>.presentationBackground</code> to a solid color. Unfortunately, it doesn't work to use the semantic <code>ShapeStyle</code> <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/shapestyle/background" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>background</code></a>. But it does work to use <code>UIColor.systemBackground</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>.sheet(isPresented: $sheetIsPresented) {
    VStack {
        // ...
    }
    .presentationBackground(Color(.systemBackground))
}
</code></pre>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/jc1GHdFd.gif" alt="Animation" /></p>

Q: Program runs, but Painter won&#39;t Paint? A: <p>You usually need something like this in another file:</p>
<pre><code>import org.code.neighborhood.*;

public class MyNeighborhood {
  public static void main(String[] args) {

    RowPainter painter = new RowPainter(0, 0, &quot;east&quot;, 10);

    painter.paint();
    painter.moveIfCanMove();
    painter.paint();
    painter.moveIfCanMove();
    painter.paint();

  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Without something like this, <strong>nothing will run</strong>.</p>
